# Vimla Responsive UI & Testing

These rules are mandatory for all frontend UI work in Vimla.

The goal is not to create a separate CSS layout for every phone or screen size.

The goal is to build fluid, resilient interfaces that remain usable across:

- small phones;
- common mobile phones;
- large phones;
- tablets;
- small laptops;
- regular laptops;
- desktop monitors;
- wide desktop monitors;
- portrait orientation;
- landscape orientation;
- touch input;
- mouse input;
- keyboard input.

"Responsive on all devices" means robust layout behavior across viewport ranges, not one hard-coded branch per physical device model.

---

# 1. Responsive behavior is mandatory

Every new user-facing page and shared UI component must be designed and implemented responsively from the beginning.

Do not implement desktop first and postpone mobile as undefined technical debt unless explicitly approved.

A feature is not complete if its core flow breaks on:

```text
mobile
tablet
laptop
desktop
```

Responsive behavior is part of feature correctness.

---

# 2. Centralized breakpoint system

Breakpoints must be centralized in the design system.

Do not invent arbitrary breakpoints in individual feature files.

Use a shared breakpoint/token layer.

Recommended initial viewport ranges may be approximately:

```text
xs: 0+
sm: 480+
md: 768+
lg: 1024+
xl: 1280+
2xl: 1536+
```

Exact values should be finalized in `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`.

Do not optimize for one specific iPhone, Android device, MacBook, or monitor.

Use content-driven responsive behavior.

---

# 3. Prefer fluid layout over breakpoint-heavy layout

Prefer:

- CSS Grid;
- Flexbox;
- `minmax()`;
- `clamp()`;
- intrinsic sizing;
- `max-width`;
- percentage/fr units;
- container constraints;
- wrapping;

before adding a new media query.

GOOD:

```scss
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 280px), 1fr));
}
```

BAD:

```scss
@media (width: 1378px) { ... }
@media (width: 1394px) { ... }
@media (width: 1440px) { ... }
```

Avoid device-specific CSS hacks.

---

# 4. No accidental horizontal overflow

Pages must not create unintended horizontal scrolling.

Always verify:

- long text;
- long URLs;
- code blocks;
- tables;
- model names;
- email addresses;
- payment IDs;
- badges;
- buttons;
- modal contents;
- sidebar layouts.

Use appropriate:

```text
min-width: 0
overflow-wrap
text-overflow
overflow-x
scroll containers
```

Do not hide important content by applying `overflow: hidden` blindly.

---

# 5. Minimum supported width

The consumer Vimla application should remain functionally usable down to approximately:

```text
320 CSS px
```

unless a feature has a documented technical reason otherwise.

Admin is desktop-first, but critical Admin flows should still remain usable on narrow screens through responsive stacking, drawers, or horizontally scrollable data containers.

Admin must not become unusable solely because the viewport is smaller than a laptop.

---

# 6. Viewport height matters too

Do not design only for width.

Support:

- short laptop screens;
- mobile browser chrome;
- on-screen keyboards;
- split-screen windows.

Avoid fixed-height layouts that cut off:

- auth submit buttons;
- chat composer;
- dialogs;
- billing actions;
- settings controls.

Prefer:

```text
min-height
dvh
svh
flex layouts
scrollable content regions
```

over assuming `100vh` always represents usable space.

---

# 7. Mobile browser viewport units

When implementing full-height mobile layouts, prefer modern viewport units such as:

```text
dvh
svh
lvh
```

where appropriate.

Do not rely only on legacy `100vh` for mobile application shells if it causes browser-toolbar or keyboard issues.

Provide sensible fallback where necessary.

---

# 8. Safe-area support

Interfaces used on mobile must account for safe-area insets where relevant.

Examples:

- bottom chat composer;
- bottom navigation;
- full-screen drawers;
- full-screen modals;
- top fixed bars.

Use:

```css
env(safe-area-inset-top)
env(safe-area-inset-right)
env(safe-area-inset-bottom)
env(safe-area-inset-left)
```

through shared layout utilities/tokens where appropriate.

Do not hardcode iPhone-specific pixel offsets.

---

# 9. Touch targets

Interactive controls on touch devices should generally have an effective target of approximately:

```text
44 × 44 CSS px
```

where appropriate.

Small visual icons may remain visually smaller, but the clickable/tappable target must remain usable.

Applies especially to:

- icon buttons;
- sidebar controls;
- close buttons;
- dropdown triggers;
- message actions;
- mobile navigation;
- password visibility controls.

---

# 10. Do not depend on hover

Hover is enhancement, not required functionality.

Any action available only through `:hover` is invalid for touch devices unless there is another obvious touch-accessible path.

Examples:

BAD:

```text
message delete appears only on hover with no mobile equivalent
```

GOOD:

```text
desktop hover actions
+
mobile overflow/action menu
```

---

# 11. Pointer capability

When interaction behavior differs by input type, prefer capability media queries where useful:

```css
@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) { ... }
```

Do not assume:

```text
wide screen = mouse
small screen = touch
```

Convertible devices exist.

---

# 12. Typography must scale safely

Typography should remain readable across viewports.

Use centralized type tokens and, where appropriate, fluid values with `clamp()`.

Do not aggressively shrink body text to make layouts fit.

Body text should remain comfortably readable on mobile.

If space is constrained:

- reflow layout;
- wrap;
- stack controls;
- collapse secondary information;

instead of reducing text to unreadable sizes.

---

# 13. Responsive spacing

Spacing should use the design-system scale.

Layouts may become denser on narrow viewports, but do not introduce random mobile-only spacing values.

Prefer explicit responsive spacing tokens or shared patterns.

Example concept:

```text
page padding:
mobile -> smaller system spacing
tablet -> medium
desktop -> larger
```

Keep the same visual rhythm.

---

# 14. Application shell behavior

The main Vimla application shell must have explicit responsive behavior.

Desktop:

```text
persistent/collapsible sidebar
main workspace
```

Tablet:

```text
compact/collapsible sidebar or drawer depending on available width
```

Mobile:

```text
sidebar becomes Drawer / Sheet
main content uses full width
```

Do not keep a fixed 280px sidebar visible on a 375px viewport.

---

# 15. Sidebar state

Desktop sidebar state and mobile navigation state must be separate concepts.

Do not let:

```text
desktop collapsed preference
```

break:

```text
mobile drawer behavior
```

The mobile drawer should close after relevant navigation and support:

- Escape;
- overlay click where appropriate;
- focus management;
- keyboard navigation.

---

# 16. Chat responsive behavior

Chat is a critical Vimla flow.

It must work well across all supported viewports.

Mobile requirements:

- conversation content uses available width;
- composer remains accessible above safe area/on-screen keyboard;
- attachments/actions do not overflow;
- model selector remains reachable;
- message actions remain touch-accessible;
- code blocks can scroll horizontally without breaking the page;
- long responses wrap safely;
- sidebar/navigation becomes drawer;
- optional secondary panels must not permanently consume screen width.

Do not merely shrink the desktop chat layout.

---

# 17. Chat composer

The composer must adapt to width.

Desktop may show:

```text
attachment
mode/model selector
input
voice
send
```

in one horizontal composition.

Mobile may:

- stack secondary controls;
- move model selector into a compact menu;
- collapse secondary actions;
- preserve a large primary text area.

The send action must remain reachable when the mobile keyboard is open.

---

# 18. Authentication responsive behavior

Auth screens must work on:

- 320px small mobile;
- common mobile;
- tablet;
- desktop;
- short viewport heights.

Do not require a large centered card with fixed height.

If a split-screen marketing layout exists:

```text
desktop -> split layout allowed
mobile -> auth form becomes primary full-width content
```

The actual sign-in/sign-up flow must remain simple.

OTP inputs must fit small screens without horizontal overflow.

---

# 19. Settings responsive behavior

Desktop Settings may use:

```text
settings navigation + content
```

On mobile:

- navigation may become a top-level list, drawer, or compact selector;
- content should use full width;
- forms should stack;
- action buttons should remain reachable.

Do not force a desktop settings sidebar into a narrow screen.

---

# 20. Billing responsive behavior

Billing/Pricing must remain understandable on mobile.

Plan cards should:

- stack cleanly;
- preserve comparison clarity;
- avoid tiny multi-column cards;
- keep price and CTA visible;
- avoid overflow from long feature names.

Payment history tables may become:

- horizontally scrollable contained tables;
- responsive cards/rows;
- column-prioritized layouts.

Do not hide critical payment status or amount.

---

# 21. Projects responsive behavior

Future Project UI must follow the same system.

Desktop may show:

```text
project navigation
main chat/workspace
optional context panel
```

Mobile must not display three permanent columns.

Use:

- tabs;
- drawers;
- sheets;
- route-level views;
- compact project header.

Core project actions must remain accessible.

---

# 22. Admin responsive behavior

Admin is desktop-first but not desktop-only.

Desktop:

- dense tables;
- filters;
- side navigation;
- charts.

Tablet/mobile:

- filters may collapse into a Drawer;
- KPI cards stack;
- tables use controlled horizontal scrolling or responsive column reduction;
- actions remain available;
- detail panels may become full-screen routes/sheets.

Never simply hide financial fields because screen width is small.

---

# 23. Tables

Tables require explicit responsive strategy.

For every data table decide which approach is appropriate:

```text
horizontal scroll container
responsive column visibility
stacked row/card representation
detail drilldown
```

Do not allow a table to stretch the entire page horizontally.

Critical identifiers, amount, status, and primary action should remain accessible.

---

# 24. Dialogs and drawers

Shared Dialog/Modal behavior:

Desktop:

```text
centered constrained dialog
```

Small mobile:

```text
may use near-full-width dialog or bottom/full-screen sheet depending on task
```

Dialogs must respect:

- viewport height;
- keyboard;
- safe area;
- internal scrolling;
- focus management.

Do not render a fixed 700px-wide dialog on a 375px device.

---

# 25. Forms

Forms must reflow intentionally.

Desktop may use two-column fields where useful.

Mobile should normally stack fields vertically.

Do not use two narrow columns simply to preserve a desktop screenshot.

Primary actions should remain clear.

For long forms, consider sticky actions only if they do not obstruct content or mobile keyboard.

---

# 26. Navigation and breadcrumbs

Breadcrumbs must not overflow narrow screens.

Use:

- truncation;
- collapsing intermediate levels;
- responsive back navigation.

Do not wrap a long breadcrumb into an unreadable multi-line block unless intentional.

---

# 27. Images and uploaded content

User-uploaded images/files must:

- fit within available width;
- preserve aspect ratio;
- not overflow containers.

Use sensible max dimensions.

Do not stretch low-resolution images.

---

# 28. Code blocks

Code blocks must not force whole-page horizontal scroll.

Use contained:

```text
overflow-x: auto
```

with visible accessible scrolling behavior.

Copy buttons must remain reachable on small screens.

---

# 29. Long content resilience

Test UI with intentionally difficult content:

- long Russian words/phrases;
- long English text;
- long email;
- long project name;
- long conversation title;
- long model name;
- large monetary values;
- long payment IDs;
- multiple badges.

Do not test only ideal short strings.

---

# 30. Localization and responsiveness

RU and EN can have different text lengths.

A layout is not responsive if it works in English but breaks in Russian, or vice versa.

Responsive tests should include both locales for critical UI where practical.

Do not use fixed widths based on one language.

---

# 31. Zoom support

Core application flows should remain usable at browser zoom levels such as:

```text
200%
```

where reasonably required for accessibility.

Do not disable zoom.

Avoid layouts that depend on exact physical pixel size.

---

# 32. Orientation

Critical mobile flows should work in both:

```text
portrait
landscape
```

Do not create landscape-only breakage from fixed-height headers/composers.

No need for a unique design for every orientation; layouts should reflow safely.

---

# 33. Responsive state must not change business semantics

Responsive UI must not change:

- permissions;
- billing;
- Usage;
- project ownership;
- payment status;
- AI mode semantics;
- Admin authorization.

Mobile UI may reorganize controls, but it must not silently remove important functionality.

---

# 34. SSR and hydration

Responsive implementation must not depend on unsafe server/client viewport assumptions that produce hydration mismatches.

Prefer CSS media queries for presentation changes.

Use JavaScript viewport detection only when actual behavior cannot reasonably be expressed in CSS.

If JS responsiveness is necessary:

- isolate it;
- handle SSR safely;
- test hydration.

---

# 35. Avoid duplicate mobile and desktop applications

Do not maintain two separate feature implementations such as:

```text
DesktopBillingPage
MobileBillingPage
```

unless there is an exceptional architectural reason.

Prefer shared data/business logic with responsive composition.

Avoid duplicating domain actions and fetching logic.

---

# 36. Shared responsive utilities

Reusable responsive behavior belongs in the design system.

Examples:

```text
Container
Stack
Inline
Cluster
ResponsiveGrid
VisuallyHidden
Drawer
```

or equivalent utilities where useful.

Do not build a new responsive helper system in each feature.

---

# 37. Responsive testing is mandatory

Critical UI flows require automated responsive coverage.

Do not consider manual browser resizing sufficient.

Use Playwright for viewport/device-level regression coverage.

The standard test suite must not use live:

- T-Bank;
- ProxyAPI;
- email/SMS providers.

Use existing mocks/test infrastructure.

---

# 38. Representative viewport matrix

Do not try to test every physical device.

Use representative viewport classes.

Minimum recommended matrix:

```text
Small Mobile
320 × 568

Mobile
375 × 667

Modern Mobile
390 × 844

Tablet Portrait
768 × 1024

Tablet / Small Laptop
1024 × 768

Laptop
1280 × 800

Desktop
1440 × 900

Wide Desktop
1920 × 1080
```

The exact matrix may be adjusted to keep CI practical, but all major layout classes must remain represented.

---

# 39. Browser coverage

Where practical, critical responsive flows should be tested in representative browser engines via Playwright:

```text
Chromium
WebKit
Firefox
```

At minimum:

- Chromium desktop;
- one mobile WebKit-style device profile or equivalent;
- critical layout smoke coverage in Firefox.

Do not multiply every test by every browser and viewport if it makes CI excessively slow.

Use a targeted matrix.

---

# 40. Playwright device emulation

Use Playwright device profiles where appropriate for realistic mobile behavior.

Examples may include a representative:

```text
iPhone
Android/Pixel
tablet
```

Do not write CSS specifically for these models.

Device profiles exist for regression testing, not implementation targeting.

---

# 41. Required responsive smoke flows

At minimum, after the design-system refactor, automated responsive smoke tests should cover:

## Consumer app

```text
Sign In
Sign Up
Verify Email
Forgot/Reset Password
Main Chat
Chat Composer
Settings
Security
Billing
Usage
```

## Future Projects

```text
Project list
Project workspace
PLAN_LOCKED state
member states
Context View
```

## Admin

```text
Admin login/strong auth
Finance Overview
Tariff editor
Payments table/detail
Users table/detail
```

Only implement tests for features that already exist.

Do not mock future screens that are not implemented yet.

---

# 42. Responsive assertions

Responsive tests should assert behavior, not only screenshots.

Examples:

```text
no unintended page-level horizontal overflow
primary action is visible/reachable
sidebar is replaced by mobile drawer
drawer can open and close
composer is visible
form submit button is reachable
dialog fits viewport
table is contained
critical labels are not clipped
navigation remains operable
```

Do not rely only on visual snapshots.

---

# 43. Horizontal overflow assertion

Create a reusable Playwright helper where practical.

For critical pages verify conceptually:

```js
document.documentElement.scrollWidth <= document.documentElement.clientWidth
```

except where the page intentionally contains a controlled internal horizontal scroller such as a code block or table.

Do not treat intentional component-level scrolling as page overflow.

---

# 44. Touch interaction tests

For mobile flows verify key actions do not require hover.

Test:

- opening mobile navigation;
- selecting model/mode;
- submitting forms;
- sending chat message;
- opening dropdown;
- closing modal/drawer;
- billing CTA.

Use touch/device emulation where practical.

---

# 45. Keyboard tests

Responsive work must not regress keyboard accessibility.

Critical desktop flows should verify:

- Tab navigation;
- focus-visible;
- Enter/Space activation;
- Escape to close overlays;
- focus return after Dialog/Drawer close.

Do not create separate inaccessible mobile components.

---

# 46. Mobile keyboard resilience

For forms/chat, implementation should account for virtual keyboards.

Automated browser emulation cannot perfectly reproduce every OS keyboard, so also provide manual verification instructions for:

- iOS Safari;
- Android Chrome;

before production release.

Composer or submit actions must not become permanently hidden behind the keyboard.

---

# 47. Visual regression tests

Use visual screenshot tests selectively.

Good candidates:

```text
UI catalog
AppShell
Auth shell
Chat empty state
Settings shell
Admin shell
```

Avoid screenshot-testing every dynamic page if it makes the suite brittle.

Behavioral responsive tests remain mandatory even when screenshots exist.

---

# 48. UI catalog responsive review

The local/test-only UI catalog such as:

```text
/dev/ui
```

should be usable at:

```text
mobile
tablet
desktop
dark mode
light mode
```

Shared component changes should be reviewed there before being accepted.

---

# 49. Test selectors

Use semantic queries first:

```text
role
label
text
```

Add stable `data-testid` only where necessary.

Do not litter UI with test IDs when accessible selectors are available.

Responsive tests should verify the same user-visible behavior users interact with.

---

# 50. CI strategy

Responsive coverage must be meaningful without making CI unreasonably slow.

Preferred strategy:

- full existing E2E on primary desktop browser;
- focused responsive smoke suite across representative viewport classes;
- targeted cross-browser tests for critical shared shells/components;
- visual snapshots for a small stable set of screens.

Do not run every full E2E scenario across 8 viewports × 3 browsers unless there is a justified reason.

---

# 51. Manual device verification before production

Automated testing does not replace final physical-device smoke testing.

Before production UI release, manually verify at least:

```text
real iPhone / iOS Safari
real Android / Chrome
desktop Safari
desktop Chromium browser
```

when access to these environments is available.

Document any unavailable environment instead of pretending it was tested.

---

# 52. Performance on mobile

Responsive design must consider lower-powered devices and slower networks.

Avoid unnecessary:

- large animation libraries;
- huge JS-only layout systems;
- excessive rerenders;
- large blocking images;
- expensive effects.

Motion should remain smooth but lightweight.

Respect reduced-motion settings.

---

# 53. Network-independent layout

The basic shell and loading states must remain structurally usable while API requests are pending.

Do not make layout dimensions depend on loaded content in a way that causes excessive shifting.

Use appropriate Skeletons/placeholders.

---

# 54. Responsive design review checklist

Before completing a frontend feature, verify manually and/or automatically:

- 320px width works;
- common mobile width works;
- tablet works;
- laptop works;
- desktop works;
- wide desktop does not stretch content excessively;
- short viewport works;
- portrait works;
- landscape does not break critical flow;
- no accidental page horizontal scroll;
- long RU text works;
- long EN text works;
- touch actions work without hover;
- keyboard navigation works;
- dialogs fit;
- inputs fit;
- composer fit is correct;
- safe areas are respected where relevant;
- reduced motion works;
- dark/light both work.

---

# 55. New shared component responsive checklist

Before adding a shared component to `@vimla/ui`, verify:

- it does not assume a fixed page width;
- it supports narrow containers;
- text can wrap/truncate intentionally;
- actions do not overflow;
- touch target is usable;
- keyboard behavior remains correct;
- mobile state does not rely on hover;
- both themes work;
- reduced motion works;
- component is demonstrated in narrow and wide states in `/dev/ui` where appropriate.

---

# 56. New page responsive checklist

Before completing a page, verify:

- page uses centralized breakpoints;
- layout is fluid between breakpoints;
- mobile is not merely a scaled desktop page;
- primary actions remain reachable;
- secondary controls collapse gracefully;
- long content cannot destroy layout;
- dialogs/dropdowns remain within viewport;
- no duplicate mobile business logic exists;
- no viewport-specific security/business behavior exists;
- responsive Playwright coverage exists for critical flow.

---

# 57. Priority order

If a visual reference conflicts with responsive requirements, use this priority:

1. security;
2. product correctness;
3. accessibility;
4. responsive usability;
5. Vimla Design System consistency;
6. visual similarity to the reference.

A generated screenshot is never a reason to ship a layout that breaks on real devices.

---

# 58. Main rule

Build one adaptive Vimla interface, not separate pixel-perfect implementations for individual devices.

Use:

**fluid layout first → centralized breakpoints second → device-specific exception only as a last resort.**

Every important Vimla flow must remain understandable and operable across mobile, tablet, laptop, and desktop.
